# Build artifacts and local dev state — the image builds these itself.
# Without these exclusions a build on a machine that ran `npm run dev`
# fails outright (tar cannot archive .next/dev's special files) and the
# context needlessly ships hundreds of MB.
node_modules
.next

# Tests, their artifacts and their fixtures. e2e is excluded whole: the
# recorded OpenHolidays fixture (e2e/fixtures/openholidays/) is ODbL data
# kept for tests only, and no snapshot of it may reach an image (RFC-014
# §5.5). e2e/licence-files.spec.ts checks this line is here.
e2e
playwright-report
test-results

# Local stack state and secrets — must never enter a build context:
# docker/data is the live Postgres bind mount, docker/.env holds the
# stack's generated secrets (COPY . . would bake both into builder layers).
docker/data
docker/.env

# Everything else in docker/ that holds an install's secrets. None of it is
# read at build time: the image takes only docker/migration*.sql and the
# entrypoint, and kong.yml and diun.yml reach their containers from the host
# through bind mounts. On an install that builds from source these files carry
# real keys (setup.sh writes them in), and `COPY . .` would bake them into a
# builder layer — as would the copies kinboard-entry.sh makes while it edits
# them (RFC-018): its temp files, the saved KINBOARD_ENTRY line, its state.
docker/.env*
docker/*.entry-tmp.*
docker/kong.yml
docker/kong.yml.*
docker/diun/diun.yml
docker/diun/*.entry-tmp.*

# Next.js env files. These are for `npm run dev` and must never reach a
# build — Next inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_* at BUILD time, so whatever is in
# .env.local is frozen into the client bundle no matter what the container
# is given at runtime.
#
# This is not hypothetical. `./setup.sh` — the documented first step for a
# self-hoster — copies .env.example to .env.local verbatim, including
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://localhost:8000. Building after that bakes
# localhost:8000 into window.__ENV, which the browser-side supabase client
# reads *ahead* of the correct runtime value. The container env is right,
# the page renders, health checks and curl all pass, and every data request
# dies on CORS against localhost:8000 — visible only as a frontend stuck
# loading. That took production down on 2026-08-06.
#
# Measured on this file's own commit: 7 files in .next/ carried
# localhost:8000 with .env.local present, 0 without it.
#
# CI never had the file (it is gitignored), which is why the published
# image was always correct and only local builds broke. Excluding it here
# makes that true for everyone rather than by accident.
.env*
!.env.example

*.log
